V['EMPIRICAL']=("Two separately written enumerations of the k-term expansions of 1 agree on F(k), on the size of D<sub>k</sub> and on v(k) for every k ≤ 6. Route A searches on the remaining fraction and reads the last two terms off the divisors of q²; route B is plain recursion over fractions. Route A alone gives F(7) = 245,765, |D<sub>7</sub>| = 83,231 and v(7) = 733. Seat P's enumeration, which uses the same two-term method, agrees on F(7) and v(7), and so do OEIS A006585 and the values on #293's thread. "
 "For k ≤ 7, every expansion satisfies n<sub>i</sub> ≤ k<sup>2<sup>i−1</sup></sup>, and F(k) ≤ k<sup>2<sup>k</sup>−1</sup> and v(k) ≤ 1 + k<sup>2<sup>k−1</sup></sup> hold: the bounds the paper and the Lean prove. The largest denominator is s<sub>k</sub> − 1 = 1, 6, 42, 1,806, 3,263,442 and 10,650,056,950,806 for k = 1 and 3–7. That is the classical bound n<sub>k</sub> ≤ s<sub>k</sub> − 1 (Curtiss 1922, recalled by Quanyu Tang on the #293 thread), which the Sylvester expansion 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/7 + ⋯ + 1/(s<sub>k</sub> − 1) attains for every k ≠ 2; our search simply agrees with it. "
 "An exact search gives N(b) for b ≤ 60, starting 1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4 at b = 2, …, 11. A search to b ≤ 110 finds the first b with N(b) = 4, 5 and 6 at 11, 17 and 79 (OEIS A097048, a different sequence from A400352 though they share terms); N(b) = 7 first occurs at 733, from our July search. The numerator b − 1 tracks v(k) more closely: the first b with N(b − 1, b) = k is 11, 17, 103 and 733 for k = 4, 5, 6 and 7, which are exactly v(4), …, v(7). "
 "Half of this is elementary. For every k, v(k) ≤ min{m : N(m − 1, m) ≥ k}, because a k-term expansion of 1 that contains 1/m leaves (m − 1)/m as a sum of k − 1 distinct unit fractions; this is van Doorn and Tang's reduction, and an open Formal Conjectures PR (#4870) formalizes it. The other inequality fails at k = 3: v(3) = 4, yet N(3, 4) = 2, because the only two-term expansion, 3/4 = 1/2 + 1/4, uses 4 itself. All of these are counts, not evidence for the asymptotic theorems.")
